**Research-level findings (written up as a proposal before fixing anything):**
- **Bell is now a data result, not just a theorem.** The site booked the quantum leg as a "construction check" plus "untested". But the 2015 loophole-free experiment measured S = 2.42 ± 0.20 (Hensen et al.), so any local substrate capped at S ≤ 2 is excluded by data. The hidden-communication escape is also constrained:
  - Bancal et al. 2012 rule out hidden influences at any finite speed.
  - Salart et al. 2008 put any such influence above 10⁴ c.
  - FUNDAMENTALS' own update rule is nearest-neighbour ("all cells… evaluate neighbor tensions"), so the substrate as defined is local and excluded.
  - The only escape left is a global, instantaneous update, which is Bohmian mechanics with a preferred time (an existing theory). I checked all five papers' abstracts before quoting them.
- **The Oort argument had the wrong mechanism.** The researcher persona was right: the boost's absolute size cancels, because lab G is measured at the same density. I committed predictions first and then ran the right statistic, the boost's change with height. 3 of 4 predictions held. Against Bovy & Rix's 68 ± 4 M☉/pc² at 1.1 kpc:

| Version of the density law | Predicted (M☉/pc²) | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Archive's own knee, no floor | 890–1,860 | excluded, about 10σ |
| With the Ω_m floor, γ = 2 | 69–81 | passes |
| With the Ω_m floor, γ = 0.489 | 48–57 | 2–3σ short |
| One free global knee (the +2843 fit) | 61–67 | passes |

  The γ = 0.489 floored result is the prediction that failed (I expected it to come out high). The site now says "Oort-excluded" only where it holds.

